WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.895
A function of two inputs can change in more than one direction

00:00:04.895 --> 00:00:10.513
A partial derivative asks what happens when one input moves while the other stays fixed

00:00:10.513 --> 00:00:14.633
On this bowl, each coordinate direction gives its own local slope.

00:00:15.933 --> 00:00:19.226
Fix y equal to one and move only x

00:00:19.226 --> 00:00:23.982
The cross-section becomes x squared plus one, so its slope is two x

00:00:23.982 --> 00:00:30.567
At x equal to one, the partial slope is two; the frozen y contribution behaves like a constant.

00:00:31.867 --> 00:00:39.322
At the point one, negative one, increasing x initially raises the surface, while increasing y initially lowers it

00:00:39.322 --> 00:00:43.049
The two partial derivatives are two and negative two

00:00:43.049 --> 00:00:48.433
A single height does not determine how the surface tilts in every direction.

00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:55.874
If both coordinates move along a path, neither partial derivative alone is the full rate

00:00:55.874 --> 00:01:02.015
Along x equals t and y equals two t, the height is five t squared

00:01:02.015 --> 00:01:05.700
Its derivative is ten t, combining both coordinate contributions.
